# Contributing

Set up the environment and find your way around with [development.md](development.md).
Code MUST follow [code-style.md](code-style.md).

## Branches

Always create a branch before starting a new feature or fix. Never commit directly to
`main`. Each branch MUST cover one feature or fix only. A small, focused pull request is
reviewed much faster than a large one that mixes unrelated changes.

Branch names MUST start with a prefix that says what the branch does:

| Prefix      | Use for                                          |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `feature/`  | New functionality                                |
| `bugfix/`   | Fixes for non-urgent bugs                        |
| `hotfix/`   | Urgent fixes for released code                   |
| `refactor/` | Code restructuring with no behavior change       |
| `docs/`     | Documentation only                               |
| `chore/`    | Tooling, dependencies, CI, and other upkeep      |
| `release/`  | The version bump for a release, and nothing else |

Example: `feature/websocket-inspection`, `bugfix/tls-handshake-timeout`, `release/v0.2.0`.

The prefix also labels the pull request, which sorts it into the release notes; see
[releasing.md](releasing.md).

## Before Opening a Pull Request

```bash
cargo fmt --all                                           # Format code
cargo clippy --all --all-features --tests -- -D warnings  # Lint (must pass)
cargo test                                                # Run tests
```

All three MUST pass. CI runs them again, along with the checks described in
[development.md](development.md#continuous-integration).

## Merging

Squash commits before merging. Each pull request MUST land on `main` as a single commit.

The pull request's title becomes that commit's subject, so it MUST read as one: imperative
mood, such as `Add websocket inspection`. The description becomes the commit body; keep
it to facts the diff cannot show.

## Repository Settings

A new repository needs these set by hand.

Under Settings → General → Pull Requests:

- Allow only squash merging: untick "Allow merge commits" and "Allow rebase merging".
- Set the squash commit message default to "Pull request title and description".

Under Settings → Rules → Rulesets, add a branch ruleset targeting `main` with:

- "Require a pull request before merging", so nothing reaches `main` without one.
- "Require status checks to pass", with the one check **CI passed**. It appears in the
  list only after CI has run once, so push a branch or open a pull request first.

**CI passed** is the last job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and fails if any job before
it failed. A pull request that touches only documentation skips the jobs that check code,
and passes. A new CI job joins the requirement by being listed in its `needs`, with no
change to these settings.
